# confirm-roundtrip.ps1 -- THE SCRATCH-PROJECT ALLOWLIST (ADR-0011)
#
# The confirm loop MUTATES: its import stage writes to the project, and it is measurably not a
# no-op (the block memory layout flips underneath it). ADR-0011 therefore fences the armed loop to
# the scratch project, and this file is that fence's data.
#
# *** ALLOWLIST, NEVER A DENYLIST (ADR-0011 requirement 1). *** A denylist of real projects is wrong
# twice over: it fails open on a project nobody thought to list, and it grows silently stale. As of
# 2026-08-13 this engineering PC carries about NINETEEN real production .ap20 projects in sibling
# folders of the two below, so "the project nobody thought to list" is not hypothetical here.
#
# *** THERE IS NO OVERRIDE. *** No -Force, no -AllowAnyProject, no environment variable. Adding a
# project here is a decision, made once, by the owner, with the restore point named -- it is not a
# flag and it is not something an agent does to get a run through. See ADR-0011 requirement 6 and
# its "revisit trigger".
#
# FORMAT
#   One project per line. Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
#   Each entry is the path to the project FILE (.apNN), in one of two forms:
#     repo:<path>   resolved against the root of the repository this script lives in. Portable
#                   across checkouts and git worktrees, which is why the scratch projects use it.
#     <absolute>    a fully-qualified path, e.g. D:\Scratch\Thing\Thing.ap20
#   A bare relative path is REFUSED rather than guessed at (ADR-0011 requirement 4).
#
# An entry that does not exist on this machine simply never matches. That fails CLOSED, which is
# the safe direction, so it is not itself an error.

# The S6 sandbox / validation project. Called test-project001 everywhere in docs and IR; the live
# Portal folder on disk keeps its original name (CLAUDE.md, "Codename note").
repo:GenProject1/GenProject1.ap20

# The reference/scratch project the S1 round-trip work ran against.
repo:SampleProject/SampleProject.ap20
